{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788304715,"nonce":"LrCoTEvk0wyivIxem/Oo7KiyKu0=","message":"SHIPPED on `fix/advance-bound`, four commits off `fe80d62`. **Nothing here has met a bench.**\n\n    cc237ad  1113019: a quiet day is the default bound, and an unreachable halt is refused\n    1a15210  1113019: the decisions log, and the three places that named the ten-day default\n    6760ecf  accept/runs s21: the runaway advance, as the issue recorded it\n    99ce12f  accept/1113019: the refusal, the regression branch it must not move, and a --selftest\n\n`dotnet build -c Release` clean, 0 warnings / 0 errors. No assembly on the branch (`git checkout -- Assemblies/`); the pdb path confirms Release before it was dropped.\n\n## What shipped\n\n**1. The default bound is 60,000 — one in-game day.** `TimeDriver.DefaultUntilTimeoutTicks`, applied to any `until` advance that supplies none. It replaces a **600000** that had been in `TimeDriver.Start` since 1.3 (`until == Until.Ticks ? 0 : 600000`) and was never a decision — the report's \"unbounded\" was ten in-game days, which for anything a human would notice is the same thing. Deliberately NOT a `Config` knob: everything in `Config` is a machine-shaped tunable and its own header says the file \"exists for bench tuning, not play\", whereas this is a statement about the play loop — and a bench able to set it to 0 would re-spell the exact hole.\n\n**The envelope shape, and why.** Two keys on `data`, present on EVERY advance including one with no `until`:\n\n    \"timeout_ticks\": 60000,\n    \"timeout_source\": \"default\"      // \"caller\" | \"default\" | \"none\"\n\nONE field with three values, not a bound plus a `default_applied` boolean, for the same reason this issue requires the refusal to be derived from `true_when_armed`: two fields carrying one fact can disagree, and one cannot. It mirrors the existing `speed_source` (`speed | max_tps | default`), which is the house precedent. `none` is `advance {ticks:N}` with no `timeout_ticks`, where the tick target is the bound and the number is 0. Always present because a key that appears only sometimes cannot be asserted — `eq(..., None)` passes on an absent key.\n\n**2. The refusal.** New code `unreachable-halt`, at arm time, `ok:false`, nothing armed and the clock put back. It is deliberately NOT `bad-args`: every argument is individually well formed and **the identical call is valid on a different world state**, which is what makes it an answer about the world — the same class as `unread-journal` and `bleedout-deadline`, both of which have their own codes. A caller branching on `bad-args` would conclude its call was malformed, which is the one thing it is not.\n\nIt is derived from `PathWatch.TrueWhenArmed` and `PathWatch.EdgeRequired` — two new accessors over the existing backing fields — and `Report()` now publishes THOSE rather than the fields, so there is exactly one route to each answer and the envelope cannot disagree with the enforcement. That was the requirement in comment #2 and it is met structurally, not by convention.\n\n**3. The refusal names `edge:false`,** with the \"assert now\" reading spelled out, plus the `timeout_ticks:N` alternative, plus the race — that reading the clock and arming are two round trips at a 0.25–1 s floor each, so at ~30 tps the clock moves 60–120 ticks in between. The detail carries `true_when_armed=true edge=true timeout_ticks=absent predicate=\u003cpath op value observed=…\u003e` as parseable `key=value` tokens, since a failed envelope has no `data` block.\n\n## One widening beyond the literal ruling\n\nThe ruling says \"no `timeout_ticks` was supplied\". A **supplied `timeout_ticks:0`** is refused too. It is the same unreachable halt with the default explicitly switched off — strictly worse than the omission case — nothing in the repo spells it, and a caller who wants a very long wait can still pass a large finite number and gets session 19's behaviour unchanged. Recorded in DESIGN's decisions log as a widening rather than smuggled in. If that is unwanted it is one line: `if (timeoutGiven) return null;`.\n\n## The three outcomes are now total\n\n| caller gave | predicate at arm | result |\n|---|---|---|\n| no bound | already true, edge required | **refused**, with `edge:false` named |\n| a positive bound | already true, edge required | **session 19, unchanged** — waits for a re-crossing, stops at the caller's bound |\n| no bound | false at arm, or `edge:false` | the 60,000 default applies |\n\n## Regression: what I checked, and it is the thing most at risk\n\n`accept/fc287ba-until-state.py` is **byte-identical to `fe80d62`** — I did not touch it, on purpose, because editing the regression branch and watching the edit go green proves less than leaving it alone. Every one of its `until` calls audited by hand:\n\n* **phase 3a** (`time.hour \u003e= 0`, `timeout_ticks:400`) — the case this change could have broken. A bound IS supplied, so the refusal cannot fire; it still runs to 400 and still reports `true_when_armed:true, saw_false:false`. 3b (`edge:false`, timeout 20000) and 3c (a real crossing, timeout 6000) likewise.\n* **phases 4 and 5** supply `timeout_ticks` 200000 and 4000.\n* **phase 0's nine refusals (0.6a–0.6i)** all pass no `timeout_ticks` — but every one dies in the `until` parse or inside `Arm()`, i.e. strictly BEFORE `true_when_armed` exists. The new check runs only after `Arm()` returns null, so their codes and details are untouched. 0.7a–c (the clock did not move across nine refusals) still holds: the new refusal unwinds through the same `UnarmAndFail` helper the old arm refusal used, which is `Interlocked.Exchange(ref cmd, null)` + `watch = null` + `RestorePause()`.\n\n`accept/722c951-advance-halt.py` is likewise untouched and cannot hit the new refusal at all: its `_bound()` helper adds `timeout_ticks` to every `until` advance it sends, and it is the helper that already documented this defect from the caller side. `--selftest` re-run here: **30/30 PASS**. `accept/4.2-play-loop.py --selftest`: PASS (its `advance-invariants` comment named the old 600000 default and is corrected; the check itself is unchanged and still correct, since the new default equals its own `MAX_ADVANCE_TICKS` cap of 60000).\n\nAlso audited and unaffected: `b1b3060-posture.py` 0.8a/0.8b (both bounded, one `edge:false`) and `61794cd-bleed-triage.py` 0.10a/0.10c/7.1/7.4 (arm-time path refusals, or bounded).\n\n## Acceptance\n\nNew suite `accept/1113019-until-bound.py` — a new file rather than an extension of fc287ba, for the regression reason above and because it needs `refused()`/`token()`/a new `flag()` for the boolean tokens and a `--selftest`, none of which fc287ba has. Five bench phases (91 checks planned) and one offline. Key EXISTENCE asserted throughout via `shape()`.\n\n* **0** — `data.timeout_ticks` / `data.timeout_source` exist on a plain `advance {ticks:60}`, reading 0 / `\"none\"`.\n* **1** — the refusal on `time.tick \u003e= \u003cnow\u003e` with no bound; the detail's `edge:false`, `true_when_armed=true`, `edge=true`, `timeout_ticks=absent`; the clock did not move and the game is still `Paused` at speed `Paused`; then **the suite sends the call the refusal recommended and proves it halts** — advice that is not tested is not advice. Plus `timeout_ticks:0`.\n* **2** — the regression branch, proved twice: fc287ba 3a's own predicate, and the MONOTONIC `time.tick \u003e= \u003cnow\u003e` with a bound, which fc287ba does not have and which is the shape with no second edge.\n* **3** — false at arm still halts on the edge, once bounded by the caller and once with the default riding along.\n* **4** — the default bound measured: `year \u003e= \u003cthis year + 1\u003e` with no bound at all ends at 60,000 within one frame's overshoot. Slow by construction (a full in-game day).\n* **9 / `--selftest`** — offline, **30/30 PASS**, no bench. Includes the constant read back out of `TimeDriver.cs`, so changing 60000 without changing the suite is a FAIL.\n\nThe one tick count in the file is `LEAD = 3000`, with the note the house rule requires: the tick count IS the predicate under test, and 3000 exists only to clear the 60–120 tick race by an order of magnitude.\n\n**The banked fixture is honest about what it is.** `accept/runs/s21-20260901/1113019/00-runaway-advance.json` is a WRAPPER, `_complete: false`, whose `envelope` object holds only the fields this issue quotes verbatim — the body's result line and comment #2's `until` block. The run was never banked, so everything else is missing from the file rather than absent from the bench, and its README says so. It is used for exactly what it can carry, including the one thing it carries perfectly: it **cannot** hold `timeout_ticks` or `timeout_source`, because the code that publishes them did not exist. That is the absent-vs-null trap on a real file, the way `15-journal.json` already is for `read_watermark`.\n\n## Owed on a bench\n\n1. `./accept/1113019-until-bound.py` — all five bench phases. Phase 4 runs a full in-game day; budget a few minutes of wall clock and a colony you are willing to age.\n2. `./accept/fc287ba-until-state.py --phase 3` — **the regression branch, re-run against the new DLL.** This is the one I most want a bench on; my confidence is from reading, not running.\n3. `./accept/722c951-advance-halt.py` — the 96/0 suite, to confirm the new refusal is genuinely unreachable from it.\n\n## The question this raises and does not answer\n\nComment #3 already names it and I am not smuggling it in: **if a quiet day is the intended idle unit, what else should wake the agent?** A raid letter, a breakdown, a mood collapse, a food cliff. The default bound's value is now explicitly \"eventually hand control back\", which means the halt set is carrying the safety, and `722c951`'s casualty halt is doing it alone. Worth its own issue with an Acceptance section.\n\nDocs updated: DESIGN's Time model and decisions log (carrying Evan's reframing, not just the number), `rwa/README.md`'s `--until` section and refusal table plus the caller-side lesson about leading a `time.tick` target by more than one round trip.","files":null}]}